 ## Overview
-Adaptive Step Size Calculation determines the distance between samples during ray marching by scaling the step size based on the viewing angle relative to the volume, specifically using a cone angle parameter [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of balancing rendering speed and visual quality in atmospheric clouds, where top-down views require fewer steps than horizon views [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard constant step ray marching by varying the step count dynamically based on geometry orientation, and from sibling methods like [Raycasting Step Size & Aspect Ratio Normalization](3734-raycasting-step-size-aspect-ratio-normalization.md) by relying on a cone-angle heuristic rather than aspect ratio normalization for step determination [S1, G, unofficial].
+Adaptive Step Size Calculation determines the distance between samples during ray marching by scaling the current ray time with a cone angle parameter and clamping the result [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of balancing rendering speed and visual quality by dynamically adjusting step size based on ray progress and viewing geometry [G]. It differs from standard constant step ray marching by varying the step size dynamically, and from sibling methods like [Raycasting Step Size & Aspect Ratio Normalization](3734-raycasting-step-size-aspect-ratio-normalization.md) by relying on a cone-angle heuristic rather than aspect ratio normalization for step determination [S1, G, unofficial].
 
